---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { APP, CHECKSUMS, DIST, DOWNLOADS, GITHUB } from '../links'
---

<Base
  title="Get Oh Hi markdown"
  description="Download the Oh Hi markdown desktop app for macOS and Linux, or open it in your browser."
>
  <h1>Get Oh Hi <em class="mk">mark</em>down</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The desktop app works on folders on your disk and updates itself. A browser is enough for
    joining a shared folder.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Desktop app</h2>
    <ul class="downloads">
      {
        DOWNLOADS.map((d) => (
          <li>
            <span class="os">{d.os}</span>
            <a href={`${DIST}/${d.file}`} download>
              {d.file}
            </a>
            <span class="note">{d.note}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      Check a download against <a href={CHECKSUMS}>SHA256SUMS.txt</a> from the same release.
    </p>

    <h3>First launch on macOS</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Open the <code>.dmg</code> and drag <strong>ohhi</strong> into Applications.</li>
      <li>
        The first launch is blocked, because the app is not notarized yet. Go to
        <strong>System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, find “ohhi was blocked…” and
        choose <strong>Open Anyway</strong>. Or, in Terminal:
        <code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/ohhi.app</code>
      </li>
      <li>Updates after that install themselves. Only the first launch needs this.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>First launch on Linux</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        The AppImage needs FUSE 2: <code>sudo apt install libfuse2</code> on Ubuntu 22.04,
        <code>libfuse2t64</code> on 24.04 and later.
      </li>
      <li><code>chmod +x ohhi_amd64.AppImage</code>, then run it. It updates in place.</li>
      <li>
        The <code>.deb</code> installs with <code>sudo apt install ./ohhi_amd64.deb</code>, but
        only updates itself when a release carries its signature. Prefer the AppImage.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>In your browser</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={APP}>app.ohhi.md</a> runs the same app. Notes you make there live in that browser’s
      storage, not in a folder, and each browser has its own copy. A shared folder also lives on
      the relay and on every device holding a link to it, so the browser is a fine place to join
      one. Keep your own notes in the desktop app.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>For agents</h2>
    <p>
      <code>ohhi</code> is the command an agent uses over a vault: ranked search that answers with
      references, a note’s outline or one section, links and backlinks, collections, what changed
      since, and journaled writes. <code>ohhi mcp</code> serves the same operations to any MCP
      client. It works on a vault in a folder on disk, so the desktop app’s, not a browser’s.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is not bundled with the app yet: it installs from a checkout of
      <a href={GITHUB}>the repository</a> (private today), with git and Node 22 on the machine.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        Clone it and install its dependencies, with the repository’s own copy of Rush:
        <code>node common/scripts/install-run-rush.js install</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Then, from that folder, <code>./scripts/install-ohhi.sh</code> builds <code>ohhi</code> and
        links it into <code>~/.local/bin</code> (have that on your <code>PATH</code>).
      </li>
      <li>
        For Claude Code, <code>./scripts/install-skill.sh</code> links the <em>ohhi-vault</em> skill
        into <code>~/.claude/skills</code>; start Claude Code with the vault named,
        <code>OHHI_VAULT="&lt;folder&gt;" claude</code>, and the skill does the rest. Or add the commands as
        MCP tools: <code>claude mcp add ohhi -- ohhi mcp --vault "&lt;folder&gt;"</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Any other MCP client gets a server entry running <code>ohhi mcp --vault "&lt;folder&gt;"</code>.
        <code>ohhi stats --vault "&lt;folder&gt;"</code> checks it sees the vault; <code>ohhi help</code> lists
        every command.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="fine">
      In the app, <strong>Settings › AI › Outside agents</strong> has these lines with the open vault’s
      folder filled in. The full guide is at <a href={`${APP}/guide/agents.html`}>Working with an agent</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
